"Figure caption: Schematic illustration of the preparation of siB/QU@L-OL and its therapeutic mechanism in Alzheimer's disease. Following intranasal administration, siB/QU@L-OL adheres to the olfactory epithelium and gains access to the brain. By leveraging the dual therapeutic effects of gene silencing (siB) and anti-inflammatory/antioxidant actions (QU), the formulation not only significantly reduces Aβ production but also remodels the pathological inflammatory and oxidative microenvironment through activation of the PI3K/Akt/Nrf2-mediated endogenous defense pathway. Ultimately, the siB/QU@L-OL mitigates cognitive dysfunction in AD mice.\n\nFigure caption: Preparation and characterization of siB/QU@L-OL. (A) Schematic of siB/QU@L-OL preparation using a microfluidic chip. (B) Agarose gel electrophoresis of naked siRNA and siB@L at different N/P ratios. (C, D) Particle size distribution (C) and zeta potential (D) of siB/QU@L and siB/QU@L-OL (n = 3). (E) TEM image of siB/QU@L-OL. Scale bar, 100 nm. (F, G) Particle size (F) and PDI (G) after incubation with proteins and enzymes at 37 °C for 24 h (n = 3). (H) TNS fluorescence assay of the apparent pKa of siB/QU@L-OL (n = 3). (I) In vitro QU release profiles of siB/QU@L-OL at different pH conditions (n = 3). (J) Viability of SH-SY5Y and N2a-APPswe cells exposed to siB/QU@L-OL for 24 h measured by CCK-8 assay (n = 5). (K) Hemolysis assay of siB/QU@L-OL (n = 6). Inset: representative image of erythrocyte hemolysis. Data are presented as mean ± SD.\n\nFigure caption: Investigation of cellular uptake and lysosomal escape of LNPs. (A) CLSM images of Cy5.5-siNC/QU@L-OL uptake and lysosomal escape in SH-SY5Y cells for 2, 4, and 8 h (blue: DAPI; red: Cy5.5; green: LysoTracker). Scale bars, 25 μm. (B) Mean fluorescence intensity (MFI) of Cy5.5 uptake at the indicated time points (n = 3). (C) Schematic illustration of lysosomal escape and QU release from LNPs. (D) Pearson's correlation coefficient of colocalization between lysosomes and LNPs. (n = 3). (E) Flow cytometry analysis of Cy5.5-siNC/QU@L-OL uptake in SH-SY5Y cells under various endocytosis inhibition. (F) Relative uptake percentages in SH-SY5Y cells pretreated with different inhibitors compared with PBS (n = 5). Data are presented as mean ± SD. ∗P < 0.05, ∗∗P < 0.01, ∗∗∗P < 0.001, ns = not significant, P > 0.05. (For interpretation of the references to color in this figure legend, the reader is referred to the Web version of this article.)\n\nFigure caption: In vitro neuroprotective effects and underlying mechanisms. (A, B) Fluorescence images of DCFH-DA staining for intracellular ROS (A) and quantitative analysis (B) (n = 3). (blue: Hoechst; green: DCFH-DA). Scale bars, 50 μm. (C) Schematic of Aβ-induced mitochondrial and neuronal damage rescued by LNPs. (D, E) JC-1 staining images for mitochondrial membrane potential (D) and quantification (E) (n = 3). (red: JC-1 aggregates; green: JC-1 monomers). Scale bars, 50 μm. (F, G) Annexin V-APC/PI flow cytometry for apoptosis (F) and quantitative statistics (G) (n = 3). (H) SOD, CAT, and GPx activity measurements (n = 4). (I) CLSM images of nuclear Nrf2 expression in SH-SY5Y cells. (blue: DAPI; green: Nrf2). Scale bars, 25 μm. (J) Representative Western blot images of Nrf2 pathway proteins. (n = 3). (K) ELISA of IL-6, IL-1β, TNF-α, and IL-4 levels in BV2 cells (n = 5). (L) Representative Western blot images of BACE1 and sAPPβ proteins in N2a-APPswe cells (n = 3). Data are presented as mean ± SD. ∗P < 0.05, ∗∗P < 0.01, ∗∗∗P < 0.001, ns = not significant, P > 0.05. (For interpretation of the references to color in this figure legend, the reader is referred to the Web version of this article.)\n\nFigure caption: Evaluation of nose-to-brain targeting efficacy in vitro and in vivo. (A) Schematic illustration of the nasal epithelium model construction in vitro (Created in figdraw.com). (B) TEER value of the nasal epithelium model during maturation (n = 4). (C, D) Flow cytometry analysis (C) and quantification (D) of LNPs uptake by SH-SY5Y cells at 12 and 36 h (n = 4). (E, F) Representative in vivo fluorescence images (E) and fluorescence quantification (F) of mice after intranasal administration of LNPs at the indicated time points (n = 3). (G, H) Representative ex vivo fluorescence images (G) and fluorescence quantification (H) of brain and trigeminal nerves collected at 12 h (n = 3). (I, J) Representative ex vivo fluorescence images (I) and fluorescence quantification (J) of tissues (heart, liver, spleen, lung, kidney) collected at 48 h (n = 3). (K) Fluorescence images of frozen brain sections (hippocampus and cortex) after intranasal LNP administration (n = 3). (blue: DAPI; red: Cy5.5). Scale bars: 100 μm (overview), 50 μm (magnified images). Data are presented as mean ± SD. ∗P < 0.05, ∗∗P < 0.01, ∗∗∗P < 0.001, ns = not significant, P > 0.05. (For interpretation of the references to color in this figure legend, the reader is referred to the Web version of this article.)\n\nFigure caption: Behavioral evaluation of LNP therapy in 8-month-old APP/PS1 mice. (A) Experimental timeline for in vivo treatment and evaluation. (B) Representative locomotor trajectories in the OFT. (C, D) Percentage of distance traveled (C) and time spent (D) in the center zone of the OFT. (E) Schematic illustration of the NOR test. (F, G) Preference index (F) and discrimination index (G) in the NOR test. (H) Schematic illustration of the Y-maze test. (I, J) Percentage of entries into (I) and time spent (J) in the novel arm. Data are presented as mean ± SD (n = 9). ∗P < 0.05, ∗∗P < 0.01, ∗∗∗P < 0.001 compared to the G2 group; #P < 0.05, ##P < 0.01, ###P < 0.001 compared to the G5 group; &P < 0.05, &&P < 0.01, &&&P < 0.001 compared to the G1 group.\n\nFigure caption: In vivo transcriptomic analysis of LNP treatment effects. (A) Volcano plot of DEGs between AD and siB/QU@L-OL groups (P < 0.05, |log2FC| ≥ 0.585). (B) Heatmap of DEG clustering. (C) GO enrichment analysis of DEGs. Biological process (BP), molecular function (MF), cellular component (CC). (D) KEGG enrichment analysis of pathways. Each group contained 5 mice.\n\nFigure caption: In vivo evaluation of multi-target therapy for AD. (A) Representative Western blot images of PI3K/Akt pathway-related proteins (n = 3). (B) Representative Western blot images of Nrf2 pathway-related proteins (n = 3). (C) Representative Western blot images of BACE1 and sAPPβ proteins (n = 3). (D) Representative fluorescence images of Aβ staining in the hippocampus and cortex. (blue: DAPI; red: Aβ). Scale bars, 200 μm. (E) Representative fluorescence images of DHE staining. (blue: DAPI; red: DHE). Scale bars, 1000 μm. (F) Heat map of IL-6, IL-1β, TNF-α, and IL-4 in the hippocampus (n = 6). (G) Representative fluorescence images of NeuN staining in the hippocampus. (blue: DAPI; green: NeuN). Scale bars, 200 μm. (H) Representative fluorescence images of Nissl staining in the hippocampus. Scale bars, 200 μm (overview), 50 μm (magnified). (For interpretation of the references to color in this figure legend, the reader is referred to the Web version of this article.)"